Dispat licensing

1. Official release binaries: MIT

The copyright holder grants the MIT license printed below for the compiled
Dispat binaries published in this project's official releases, including the
Dispat executables in its official container images. For this binary grant,
"Software" means those executables in compiled form. It does not include the
corresponding source files, the CCME specification, or other GPL material in
source or document form. This grant does not change third-party license terms
for dependencies or other contents of a container image.

2. Source referencing GPL material: GPL-3.0-or-later

Dispat source files that reference or incorporate GPL-covered specification
text, algorithms or proofs are licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later. This includes
source references to sections of the CCME specification. These files carry
SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later notices. The MIT grant below does not
apply to their source form. Removing a reference or notice does not grant an
MIT license to that source.

The CCME specification and its accompanying material in specs/ccme-spec/ are
also licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later. The full GNU General Public License text
is in specs/ccme-spec/LICENSE, with copies in services/dispat/ and pkg/models/.

The CCME parser in pkg/ccme/ is expressly excluded from this source rule and
remains MIT-licensed, including its specification references. Merely importing
or calling that MIT parser does not designate another source file as GPL.
License notices and navigation links alone are not substantive specification
references under this policy.

3. Other source: MIT

Dispat source files without GPL-covered material or substantive references to
it remain under the MIT license below. File-level GPL notices take precedence
over a package's default MIT grant. Third-party code, fonts and other material
retain their own license notices and terms.

These grants distinguish official compiled binaries from source files. They
do not offer MIT as an alternative for GPL-designated source. Previously
distributed copies retain the licenses under which they were distributed.
The MIT grants for other files do not override applicable GPL requirements
when those files are combined with GPL-covered source.

